:Namespace MoveGeneration
    ⍝ Generation of pseudo-legal moves

    ⎕IO ⎕ML←1                                       
 
    generatePseudoLegalMoves←{
        ⍝ ⍺ ←→ lookup tables
        ⍝ ⍵ ←→ bitboards, game state
        ⍝ ← ←→ matrix of (from to promotion); promotion is 0 for a
        ⍝      non-promoting move, otherwise the piece type promoted to
        
        lookups←⍺
        (bitboards game_state)←⍵
        P←##.Pieces
        side←game_state[##.GameState.side]
        offset←6×side
        own←∨⌿bitboards[offset+⍳6;]
        enemy←∨⌿bitboards[(6-offset)+⍳6;]
        occupied←own∨enemy

        ⍝ One attack matrix for all non-pawn pieces: a row per piece,
        ⍝ a column per square, in the same order as from. ⍸ lists pieces
        ⍝ grouped by type, and key keeps that order.
        types←P.(N B R Q K)
        (type from)←↓⍉↑⍸bitboards[offset+types;]
        attacks←⊃⍪/type{⊂lookups generatePieceAttacks types[⍺]⍵ occupied}⌸from
        piece_moves←from expand attacks∧⍤1⊢~own

        pawn_moves←lookups generatePseudoLegalPawnMoves bitboards occupied enemy side offset (game_state[##.GameState.en_passant_square])

        rights←game_state[##.GameState.castling_rights][(2×side)+⍳2]
        castling_moves←lookups generateCastlingMoves bitboards occupied side rights

        (piece_moves,0)⍪pawn_moves⍪castling_moves
    }

    generateCastlingMoves←{
        ⍝ ⍺ ←→ lookup tables
        ⍝ ⍵ ←→ bitboards, occupancy, side to move,
        ⍝      castling rights of the side to move (kingside queenside)
        ⍝ ← ←→ matrix of (from to promotion), as for generatePseudoLegalMoves

        (bitboards occupied side rights)←⍵
        ~∨/rights:0 3⍴0
        rank←56×side
        king←rank+5

        ⍝ List the relevant squares.
        ⍝ Kingside then queenside, in the order of rights.
        to←rank+7 3
        between←rank+(6 7)(2 3 4)
        crossed←rank+6 4
        
        ⍝ No pieces between king and rook.
        clear←{~∨/occupied[⍵]}¨between
        
        ⍝ King is not in check and all squares which the king crosses are not attacked.
        ⍝ As for all pseudo legal moves, the legality filter checks if castling leaves the king in check.
        attacked←⍺ checkSquareAttacked bitboards(king,crossed)(1-side)
        safe←(~⊃attacked)∧~1↓attacked
        (king,⍪(rights∧clear∧safe)/to),0
    }

    expand←{
        ⍝ ⍺ ←→ from squares, one per row of ⍵
        ⍝ ⍵ ←→ boolean matrix, (≢⍺)×64; 1 marks a reachable square
        ⍝ ← ←→ matrix of (from to), one row per 1 in ⍵
        hits←↑⍸⍵
        ⍺[hits[;1]],⍪hits[;2]
    }

    generatePseudoLegalPawnMoves←{
        ⍝ ⍺ ←→ lookup tables
        ⍝ ⍵ ←→ bitboards, occupancy, enemy occupancy, side to move,
        ⍝      bitboard offset of the side to move, en passant square
        ⍝ ← ←→ matrix of (from to promotion), as for generatePseudoLegalMoves

        (bitboards occupied enemy side offset en_passant_square)←⍵
        
        P←##.Pieces


        pawn_squares←⍸bitboards[offset+P.P;]
        pawn_direction←8-16×side
        pawn_home_rank←2+5×side
        ⍝ en_passant is all zeros when there is no en passant square.
        en_passant←en_passant_square=⍳64

        single_push_from←pawn_squares/⍨~occupied[pawn_squares+pawn_direction]
        double_push_from←single_push_from/⍨pawn_home_rank=⌈single_push_from÷8
        double_push_from←double_push_from/⍨~occupied[double_push_from+2×pawn_direction]
        captures←⍺.pawn[1+side;pawn_squares;]∧⍤1⊢enemy∨en_passant

        pawn_moves←single_push_from,⍪single_push_from+pawn_direction
        pawn_moves⍪←double_push_from,⍪double_push_from+2×pawn_direction
        pawn_moves⍪←pawn_squares expand captures

        ⍝ A pawn reaching rank 1 or 8 yields one move per promotion piece.
        promotions←1 8∊⍨⌈pawn_moves[;2]÷8
        promoting←(pawn_moves⌿⍨4×promotions),(4×+/promotions)⍴offset+P.(Q R B N)
        pawn_moves←promoting⍪(~promotions)⌿pawn_moves,0

        pawn_moves
        
    }

    generatePieceAttacks←{
        ⍝ ⍺ ←→ lookup tables
        ⍝ ⍵ ←→ piece type, from squares, occupancy
        ⍝ ← ←→ boolean matrix, one row per from square, one column per square
        
        (piece squares occupied)←⍵
        P←##.Pieces
        L←##.LookupTables
        piece=P.N:⍺.knight[squares;]
        piece=P.K:⍺.king[squares;]
        piece=P.B:⍺ generateSlidingAttacks squares L.diagonal occupied
        piece=P.R:⍺ generateSlidingAttacks squares L.orthogonal occupied
        piece=P.Q:⍺ generateSlidingAttacks squares L.(orthogonal,diagonal)occupied
    }

    generateSlidingAttacks←{
        ⍝ ⍺ ←→ lookup tables
        ⍝ ⍵ ←→ from squares, ray directions, occupancy
        ⍝ ← ←→ boolean matrix, one row per from square, one column per square
        ⍝ Each ray ends at its first occupied square, which stays reachable
        ⍝ so that captures are included; own pieces are removed by the caller.
        (squares directions occupied)←⍵
        ⍝ Each on an empty array calls its operand on the prototype, whose
        ⍝ zeros are not valid squares.
        0=≢squares:0 64⍴0
        reach←{⍵/⍨~¯1↓0,∨\occupied[⍵]}¨⍺.rays[directions;squares]
        ⍉(⍳64)∘.∊,⌿reach
    }

    checkSquareAttacked←{
        ⍝ ⍺ ←→ lookup tables
        ⍝ ⍵ ←→ bitboards, squares, attacking side (0 white, 1 black)
        ⍝ ← ←→ boolean vector, one item per square; 1 if any piece of
        ⍝      the attacking side attacks that square
        
        (bitboards squares side)←⍵
        squares←,squares
        P←##.Pieces
        L←##.LookupTables
        attacker←bitboards[(6×side)+⍳6;]
        occupied←∨⌿bitboards

        ⍝ Attacks are symmetric: a piece on s attacks t exactly when the
        ⍝ same piece on t attacks s. Each square therefore looks outward
        ⍝ with every piece type and checks for an attacker of that type.
        hits←⍺.knight[squares;]∧⍤1⊢attacker[P.N;]
        hits∨←⍺.king[squares;]∧⍤1⊢attacker[P.K;]
        hits∨←(⍺ generateSlidingAttacks squares L.diagonal occupied)∧⍤1⊢∨⌿attacker[P.(B Q);]
        hits∨←(⍺ generateSlidingAttacks squares L.orthogonal occupied)∧⍤1⊢∨⌿attacker[P.(R Q);]
        
        ⍝ Pawns are the exception, as they attack forward only; the
        ⍝ defending side's pawn table gives the reverse direction.
        hits∨←⍺.pawn[2-side;squares;]∧⍤1⊢attacker[P.P;]
        ∨/hits
    }
 
    perft←{
        ⍝ Performance test: counts the leaf nodes of the legal move tree
        ⍝ to a fixed depth. Comparing the counts with published reference
        ⍝ values verifies move generation.
        ⍝ ⍺ ←→ lookup tables
        ⍝ ⍵ ←→ depth, bitboards, game state
        ⍝ ← ←→ number of leaf positions reachable in exactly depth plies

        lookups←⍺
        (depth bitboards game_state)←⍵

        ⍝ At depth 0 the position itself is the only leaf.
        depth=0:1
        legal_moves←lookups generateLegalMoves bitboards game_state

        0=≢legal_moves:0
        
        ⍝ At depth 1 each legal move is one leaf, so the moves need not be made.
        depth=1:≢legal_moves
        +/lookups∘perft¨(depth-1),¨↓bitboards game_state∘makeMove⍤1⊢legal_moves
    }

    makeMove←{
        ⍝ ⍺ ←→ bitboards, game state
        ⍝ ⍵ ←→ move (from to promotion), as from generatePseudoLegalMoves
        ⍝ ← ←→ bitboards, game state after the move
        (bitboards game_state)←⍺
        (from to promotion)←⍵
        P←##.Pieces
        G←##.GameState
        side←game_state[G.side]
        offset←6×side
        piece←⊃⍸bitboards[;from]
        pawn←piece=offset+P.P
        capture←∨/bitboards[;to]

        ⍝ An en passant capture removes the pawn behind the destination.
        en_passant←pawn∧to=game_state[G.en_passant_square]
        captured←to-en_passant×8-16×side
        bitboards[;from,to,captured]←0
        bitboards[piece promotion⊃⍨1+promotion≠0;to]←1

        ⍝ Castling is the only king move of two files; the rook moves
        ⍝ to the square the king crosses.
        castling←(piece=offset+P.K)∧2=|to-from
        rook_from_to←from+((1+to>from)⊃¯4 3),(to-from)÷2
        bitboards[offset+P.R;castling/rook_from_to]←castling/0 1

        ⍝ A right is lost once its king or rook square is left or
        ⍝ entered: by a king move, a rook move, or a rook capture.
        home←(5 8)(5 1)(61 64)(61 57)
        game_state[G.castling_rights]∧←~∨/¨home∊¨⊂from to
        game_state[G.en_passant_square]←(pawn∧16=|to-from)×(from+to)÷2
        game_state[G.halfmove]←(~pawn∨capture)×1+game_state[G.halfmove]
        game_state[G.fullmove]+←side
        game_state[G.side]←~side
        bitboards game_state
    }

    generateLegalMoves←{
        ⍝ ⍺ ←→ lookup tables
        ⍝ ⍵ ←→ bitboards, game state
        ⍝ ← ←→ matrix of (from to promotion); promotion is 0 for a
        ⍝      non-promoting move, otherwise the piece type promoted to

        lookups←⍺
        (bitboards game_state)←⍵
        
        pseudo_legal_moves←lookups generatePseudoLegalMoves bitboards game_state
        0=≢pseudo_legal_moves:0 3⍴0

        legal←lookups bitboards game_state∘checkMoveIsLegal⍤1⊢pseudo_legal_moves
        legal⌿pseudo_legal_moves
    }

    checkMoveIsLegal←{
        ⍝ A move is legal when, after it is made, the moving side's
        ⍝ king is not attacked.
        ⍝ ⍺ ←→ lookup tables, bitboards, game state
        ⍝ ⍵ ←→ move
        ⍝ ← ←→ 1 if the move does not leave the moving side's king attacked

        (lookups bitboards game_state)←⍺
        move←⍵

        side←game_state[##.GameState.side]
        king←(6×side)+##.Pieces.K

        bitboards_after←⊃bitboards game_state makeMove move

        ~⊃lookups checkSquareAttacked bitboards_after (⍸bitboards_after[king;])(~side)
    }
:EndNamespace
 